KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Drug repurposing
KW  - HCC
KW  - Machine learning
AB  - Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is a prevalent type of primary liver cancer that arises due to the malignant transformation of hepatocytes. HCC is characterized by a high recurrence rate, and the treatment landscape of HCC offers limited curative options, making it one of the most lethal forms of cancer globally. Drug repurposing represents a valuable strategy that involves finding new medical indications for existing approved drugs and accelerates the drug development timeline for HCC. The deployment of artificial intelligence (AI) in drug repurposing enhances the speed and accuracy of identifying potential drug candidates. The AI-driven models aid in the prediction of drug-disease association by utilizing diverse biological data inputs, including genomic, transcriptomic and clinical data. This chapter will explore how AI and machine learning (ML) techniques are employed to repurpose drugs for the treatment of HCC. Specifically, ML models, deep learning (DL) models, and open-source tools will be highlighted, which are used to predict drug-target interactions (DTIs), data extraction, and molecular descriptors calculation. The incorporation of an AI-powered repurposing method in multiple aspects of the HCC treatment landscape is discussed, emphasizing a holistic approach to obtain improved patient outcomes. The primary focus of this chapter will be on the application of AI in analyzing genetic as well as other omics data to target relevant proteins and pathways implicated in HCC. The integration of AI and drug repurposing strategies can unveil new therapeutic possibilities and address the challenges inherent to HCC for more effective treatments. This chapter will provide insights into current AI-driven drug repurposing methodologies, highlight key advances, and propose future directions for research and clinical applications in HCC treatment.
ER  - 

