AB  - Membrane receptors that bind ligands and mediate cellular signal transduction are essential for the functioning of biological cells. These proteins have also been implicated in numerous human diseases and are important drug targets. To the undergraduate and graduate student, an introductory class in computational studies of membrane receptors can illustrate highly interdisciplinary research at the interface between physics, computer science, and biology. This chapter presents a brief introduction to bioinformatics and molecular modeling studies of membrane receptors using as model systems three distinct class A G protein–coupled receptors that function as photosensors, chemokine receptors, and proton sensors. The topics we discuss could be part of a practical computer class that introduces the students o the basics of the amino acid sequence and three-dimensional structure of membrane receptors, structural modeling, and structure–function relationships.
