AB  - G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) represent the largest family of membrane proteins in the humangenome and are important drug targets for a variety of diseases. However, a significant portion of GPCRs remain “undruggable” using traditional small molecule approaches. This article explores the evolving landscape of drug discovery modalities that are opening new avenues for targeting these challenging GPCRs. We discuss the limitations of conventional small molecule drugs and delve into the potential of novel modalities, including biased ligands, allosteric modulators, peptides, macrocycles, targeted protein degradation, antibodies, antibody-drug conjugates, peptide-drug conjugates, and RNA-based approaches. By reviewing recent advances and highlighting the advantages and challenges of each modality, we provide a comprehensive overview of the strategies being employed to drug the undruggable GPCR targets and their potential to revolutionize the future of drug discovery.
ER  - 

